# nvfbc-relay policy replay fixture
# Avatar 90 fps base with frame generation x2 (180 fps displayed), -src 90, b:flip present, -lock -lag 75 -dejit. One benchmark test, no fade at either end. The base cannot hold 90 here: real frames arrive at 87 per second with a period spread of 9.2 to 12.0 ms, the M=2 comb engages a fifth of the time, and the target's phase wanders instead of alternating.
# units: microseconds. first value absolute, rest are deltas.
description Avatar 90 fps base with frame generation x2 (180 fps displayed), -src 90, b:flip present, -lock -lag 75 -dejit. One benchmark test, no fade at either end. The base cannot hold 90 here: real frames arrive at 87 per second with a period spread of 9.2 to 12.0 ms, the M=2 comb engages a fifth of the time, and the target's phase wanders instead of alternating.
# What the RELAY did on this capture. The replay model is only trustworthy
# for a configuration where it reproduces these; the test checks that.
field_worst_run 1
field_long_runs 0
field_synth_pct 9.0
src_hint 90.0
# min_placed_pct: 88.8% of captures anchored to a flip, 11.1% with no anchor. The source is a
# 180 Hz stream the base cannot hold (batch period 9.2 to 12.0 ms around 11.1) and the relay
# catches 154 of its 180 flips a second, so one capture in nine has no flip it can be placed on.
# A property of this source, not of the join; the steady 90x1 run anchors 99.9%.
min_placed_pct 88
# Replay 9.9% / runs 0 / worst 3 / re-seeds 0 against field 9.0% / runs 0 / worst 1. The blend
# share is a third of the 90x1 steady fixture's because the phase wanders instead of
# alternating: the comb engaged on 22% of presents in this test, so only the presents that
# happen to land within the bare threshold of a midpoint blend. Content steps are 1.5 periods on
# 30% of presents against 56% at 90x1 and the ideal 100%.
max_worst_run 3
max_long_runs 0
max_synth_pct 10.3
min_reseeds 0
arrivals 4002
